当DSA的候选人实际上当选后，他们就必须在党内体制内运作。

589
00:32:20,500 --> 00:32:27,340
Party. I mean, if you're an intraparty winner, are your hopes and dreams just dashed as soon
我意思是，如果你是党内获胜者，你的希望和梦想一旦上任就立刻破灭了吗？

590
00:32:27,340 --> 00:32:28,340
as you take office?
一旦你上任呢？

591
00:32:28,340 --> 00:32:33,980
Yeah, this is something I asked Ruth about. How do you walk that balance of working inside
是的，这是我问露丝的问题。你该如何在党内运作与坚持理想之间取得平衡？

592
00:32:33,980 --> 00:32:38,340
of a system that you're maybe trying to change?
在一个你或许试图改变的体制里？

593
00:32:38,340 --> 00:32:44,560
I mean, one thing that I do think is interesting about folks like Mandani or really any person
我意思是，关于像曼达尼这样的人，或者任何一位

594
00:32:44,560 --> 00:32:50,580
who's running sort of outside the box is they're going to have to govern with people who are
在体制外另辟蹊径的人，他们最终都得和那些在体制内运作的人一起执政。

595
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:52,420
operating inside the box.
在体制内运作的人。

596
00:32:52,420 --> 00:32:58,580
And so you create a potentially challenging situation where you promised voters or maybe
于是你制造了一个可能具有挑战性的局面，因为你向选民承诺过，或者也许

597
00:32:58,580 --> 00:33:01,340
you haven't promised them, but you sort of conveyed to voters that you're going to do
你并没有向他们承诺，但你向选民暗示了你会做出改变

598
00:33:01,340 --> 00:33:02,340
things differently.
事情会有所不同。

599
00:33:02,340 --> 00:33:05,660
And so you're committed to doing that, but you have to work with a bunch of people who
因此你承诺要这么做，但你必须与一群并不支持改变的人合作

600
00:33:05,660 --> 00:33:07,620
are not committed to doing things differently.
他们并不支持事情发生改变。

601
00:33:07,620 --> 00:33:11,860
And so invariably, you're going to make compromises and you're not going to be able to do all
因此，你终究会做出妥协，而你无法做到所有

602
00:33:11,860 --> 00:33:12,860
the things that you wanted.
你原本想要做的事情。

603
00:33:12,860 --> 00:33:15,020
Do you have an example of this?
你有这个例子吗？

604
00:33:15,020 --> 00:33:16,180
I do.
我有。

605
00:33:16,180 --> 00:33:22,900
So very recently in New York City, which is facing a very tight budget, I have to say,
最近在纽约市，财政状况非常紧张，我必须说，

606
00:33:22,900 --> 00:33:28,500
recently elected mayor Zoran Mamdani, who supported teachers and unions in his campaign,
最近当选的市长佐兰·马姆达尼，在竞选期间支持教师和工会，

607
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:35,500
sued to block a bill that would give $10,000 to teacher aides, an act of his that directly
试图阻止一项将1万美元发给教师助理的法案，这是他直接

608
00:33:35,500 --> 00:33:39,860
opposes the city council and the very powerful teachers union.
反对市议会和强大的教师工会的行动。

609
00:33:39,860 --> 00:33:44,260
A spokesperson for the mayor said, quote, we will not allow the political process to
市长的一位发言人表示，‘我们将不允许政治过程去

610
00:33:44,260 --> 00:33:47,100
replace the collective bargaining table.
取代集体谈判桌。

611
00:33:47,100 --> 00:33:52,180
And so then the challenge becomes conveying to voters that you are still serious and committed
所以，挑战在于向选民传达，你依然严肃且坚定。

612
00:33:52,180 --> 00:33:56,660
to the things you said you were and that the whole system isn't broken just because you've
对你说过的事情依然坚持，而且整个系统并不因此就崩溃了。

613
00:33:56,660 --> 00:34:02,460
had to do things that aren't exactly what you had promised because that's any kind of
不得不做出一些与你曾承诺的并不完全一致的决定，因为这本身就是某种

614
00:34:02,460 --> 00:34:05,940
collective decision-making system requires compromise.
集体决策体系所必需的妥协。

615
00:34:05,940 --> 00:34:09,260
And I think, you know, this is important for students
我认为，这一点对学生们来说很重要。

616
00:34:09,260 --> 00:34:12,340
to appreciate that, you know,
要理解这一点，你知道吗，

617
00:34:12,340 --> 00:34:14,140
you're not always gonna get everything you want.
你并不总是能拿到所有你想要的东西。

618
00:34:14,140 --> 00:34:15,540
And I think in our current moment
我认为在我们当前的时刻

619
00:34:15,540 --> 00:34:16,820
where it feels like some people
人们感觉似乎有些人

620
00:34:16,820 --> 00:34:18,900
are getting exactly what they want,
得到了他们想要的

621
00:34:18,900 --> 00:34:20,360
it's hard to explain, you know,
很难解释，你知道吗

622
00:34:20,360 --> 00:34:22,180
how do we know that that's happening
我们如何知道这正在发生

623
00:34:22,180 --> 00:34:25,060
or what are we not seeing all of the things
或者我们看不到所有的事情

624
00:34:25,060 --> 00:34:26,660
that they did want, that they couldn't get,
他们想要却得不到的东西

625
00:34:26,660 --> 00:34:28,140
that they took off the table.
这些事情被从选项中排除了。

626
00:34:28,140 --> 00:34:30,140
And so I think thinking through
因此我认为，我们应当思考

627
00:34:30,140 --> 00:34:33,320
as educated political observers,
作为有教育背景的政治观察者

628
00:34:33,320 --> 00:34:35,220
what are the things we're not observing?
我们到底在观察什么？

629
00:34:35,220 --> 00:34:38,400
What are the things that people have made strategic choices
人们做出了哪些战略选择

630
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:40,620
about to do, to not to do,
关于该做、该不做

631
00:34:40,620 --> 00:34:42,880
and what should we infer from all of that
以及我们应该从这些情况中推断出什么

632
00:34:42,880 --> 00:34:44,220
about our political system?
关于我们政治体系的什么？

633
